  prose(<<~'CANONICAL_PROSE', origin: :published)
    ## Role and Institutional Significance
    
    Senior Signatory of the Aureate, the merchant guild based at The Still Pools — the geothermally heated settlement at the Berg's far edge. Ingnao built Aureate during the Clever Ice Age from what the summary record calls "ledger-work and desperation," a characterization the surviving documentation supports. He authenticated the original Stillness-Bound charter, a foundational instrument of factional governance whose amended precedents still underpin treaties across the Berg. His trade-seal — applied crooked on the wax, always crooked, a detail too consistent to be accidental — accompanied official dispatches certifying fire-core output and factional transfers, including the 642-unit certification during the Ravium trade restructuring that transferred administrative control from The Cell to The Midnight Claws.
    
    His partnership with Ledger-Warden Shumei ehua'Frost, a merchant-assessor of the Aureate, appears to have been the guild's operational spine. The two never spoke publicly of Aureate's founding or of a shipment that vanished into the deep — an incident the records reference but do not explain. Shumei nonetheless set aside the finest fire-cores for his accounts first, and Ingnao marked her name in the memorial-ice every solstice, though he refused to identify for whom.
    
    ## The Hearth Plaza Cascade
    
    When the Void Matrix — a confined artifact undergoing routine assessment — consumed aurora-light from seven merchants and collapsed The Hearth Plaza's illumination system for six hours, Ingnao authorized the southern gallery's permanent sealing and recommended deep-vault containment. He spent three days managing the aftermath before handing Shumei the paperwork for her next assessments. The efficiency is characteristic.
    
    ## The Aurora-Surge and Disappearance
    
    Three winters before his disappearance, an aurora-surge at The Still Pools reportedly caused Ingnao's shadow to move in ways traders found difficult to describe and impossible to forget. He afterward took to wearing his hood low, even in still-light. The summary record states the aurora "witnessed something in him." I note this without interpretation.
    
    Ingnao has since, in the careful phrasing of a retired Ledger-Warden, "passed beyond anyone's reach." Whether this denotes death, the Walking, or some other departure, the record does not clarify. What it does clarify is consequence: substandard fire-core batches now pass Aureate certification that Ingnao would have rejected. The guild's ledgers, the retired Warden observed, no longer balance.
    
    The calluses on his flippers — noted in every physical description — suggest he did the work himself long after his title no longer required it. This is the detail I find I cannot set aside.
